[{:db/id 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c7df86f0-3809-56f0-adcd-fb286616dfa3" "question-3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" "question-5213d5bf-5843-5c8c-8790-014b534c60aa" "question-75809447-c869-5206-aa76-8147e22ce3d8" "question-b743d271-bfde-5d80-a2b4-b7cbdaa6f1f5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166485 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](images/41/4152d997be216db1a67b5f55ee112d1008ea7399e57f699ac62ba03654bd7fd5.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4c6d18b-d618-53cd-9fce-a599b57a0c5a" "question-6480541e-888b-58cb-a4eb-1a531ae87114"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-49459c91-bfac-5582-aa3d-bc1bfea60a7b" "question-aa928e5a-70af-5b00-a980-606761f10bda" 17592186167131 "question-2f0f5aff-a329-55d0-a382-ebd7a7992774"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c7df86f0-3809-56f0-adcd-fb286616dfa3" :question/id #uuid "c7df86f0-3809-56f0-adcd-fb286616dfa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161204" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{{({y}^{2})}^{2}+(2x)^{2}+(-z)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,xy,\\,{y}^{2}-1⟩\\cdot ⟨{y}^{2},\\,2x,\\,-z⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}y-z({y}^{2}-1)}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}y+z}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2190c13e-d51f-542e-bd67-2b30e8f5c940"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2190c13e-d51f-542e-bd67-2b30e8f5c940" :answer-field/id #uuid "2190c13e-d51f-542e-bd67-2b30e8f5c940" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7232adec-c36a-56bf-ab40-f21a586d8c25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c99d7a5-4c32-540a-9715-3adff1719111" "answer-ffa4bbee-939a-5f5a-a82e-c6752d30386c" "answer-7232adec-c36a-56bf-ab40-f21a586d8c25" "answer-372a4ff1-3939-5a9d-945d-86bafcb4f341" "answer-56bb391e-d6f2-5324-8833-628a5044bcbd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c99d7a5-4c32-540a-9715-3adff1719111" :answer/id #uuid "6c99d7a5-4c32-540a-9715-3adff1719111" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffa4bbee-939a-5f5a-a82e-c6752d30386c" :answer/id #uuid "ffa4bbee-939a-5f5a-a82e-c6752d30386c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}-{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7232adec-c36a-56bf-ab40-f21a586d8c25" :answer/id #uuid "7232adec-c36a-56bf-ab40-f21a586d8c25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-372a4ff1-3939-5a9d-945d-86bafcb4f341" :answer/id #uuid "372a4ff1-3939-5a9d-945d-86bafcb4f341" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56bb391e-d6f2-5324-8833-628a5044bcbd" :answer/id #uuid "56bb391e-d6f2-5324-8833-628a5044bcbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" :question/id #uuid "3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-161202" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2z+0-3(z-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y-z}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{3y-z}{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}3y-z\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-396f5c74-b8d5-5eee-bb2c-03c6070bb704"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-396f5c74-b8d5-5eee-bb2c-03c6070bb704" :answer-field/id #uuid "396f5c74-b8d5-5eee-bb2c-03c6070bb704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" "answer-2de3a55c-a3c2-5a9a-a812-bcdfecfb6feb" "answer-69182493-5072-5c9f-a5f1-7e563c775c6b" "answer-c4ece12f-0d28-5947-8017-589e0f4fd627" "answer-60427d20-92ba-5d11-ad42-138a2d763370"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" :answer/id #uuid "f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3y-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2de3a55c-a3c2-5a9a-a812-bcdfecfb6feb" :answer/id #uuid "2de3a55c-a3c2-5a9a-a812-bcdfecfb6feb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2y-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69182493-5072-5c9f-a5f1-7e563c775c6b" :answer/id #uuid "69182493-5072-5c9f-a5f1-7e563c775c6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3y+\\frac{z}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4ece12f-0d28-5947-8017-589e0f4fd627" :answer/id #uuid "c4ece12f-0d28-5947-8017-589e0f4fd627" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+4z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60427d20-92ba-5d11-ad42-138a2d763370" :answer/id #uuid "60427d20-92ba-5d11-ad42-138a2d763370" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5213d5bf-5843-5c8c-8790-014b534c60aa" :question/id #uuid "5213d5bf-5843-5c8c-8790-014b534c60aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161211" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{(-3z)^{2}+(x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{9{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨{x}^{2},\\,2xz,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨-3z,\\,x,\\,2y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{x}^{2}z+2{x}^{2}z+2y(x+y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2xy+2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-662e48f4-a2f4-58ca-946c-9c7fd0f8d49d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-662e48f4-a2f4-58ca-946c-9c7fd0f8d49d" :answer-field/id #uuid "662e48f4-a2f4-58ca-946c-9c7fd0f8d49d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea827a58-c8d0-530c-aa8a-a0149d25105d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe0f0d6e-3bb7-580f-b6e6-18fcc86988ff" "answer-b0e05106-52da-5edd-b6ad-f2bf96a94e63" "answer-ea827a58-c8d0-530c-aa8a-a0149d25105d" "answer-9c959028-5450-5d3b-9b24-6daaa18a88b9" "answer-ceabd340-e0b0-5970-af89-cfe6906a0509"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe0f0d6e-3bb7-580f-b6e6-18fcc86988ff" :answer/id #uuid "fe0f0d6e-3bb7-580f-b6e6-18fcc86988ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+2y+3z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0e05106-52da-5edd-b6ad-f2bf96a94e63" :answer/id #uuid "b0e05106-52da-5edd-b6ad-f2bf96a94e63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea827a58-c8d0-530c-aa8a-a0149d25105d" :answer/id #uuid "ea827a58-c8d0-530c-aa8a-a0149d25105d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c959028-5450-5d3b-9b24-6daaa18a88b9" :answer/id #uuid "9c959028-5450-5d3b-9b24-6daaa18a88b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ceabd340-e0b0-5970-af89-cfe6906a0509" :answer/id #uuid "ceabd340-e0b0-5970-af89-cfe6906a0509" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-75809447-c869-5206-aa76-8147e22ce3d8" :question/id #uuid "75809447-c869-5206-aa76-8147e22ce3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161208" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\boxed{}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{(xy)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,xy,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨xy,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xyz-xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xyz+2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e88ac117-843b-5a55-b610-415191bfff7f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e88ac117-843b-5a55-b610-415191bfff7f" :answer-field/id #uuid "e88ac117-843b-5a55-b610-415191bfff7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1f51faf-c287-515f-9df7-2b6d99509772" :answer-field/choices ["answer-319237d3-bef2-5a87-9ee7-4240f5045911" "answer-ca025449-86ec-5c0d-a704-da1d0fa9dc55" "answer-f1f51faf-c287-515f-9df7-2b6d99509772" "answer-967a8907-2231-5e99-a474-67c6bc6bd9f3" "answer-c5b43aea-006a-5e99-8f2a-f05fd804cb3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-319237d3-bef2-5a87-9ee7-4240f5045911" :answer/id #uuid "319237d3-bef2-5a87-9ee7-4240f5045911" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca025449-86ec-5c0d-a704-da1d0fa9dc55" :answer/id #uuid "ca025449-86ec-5c0d-a704-da1d0fa9dc55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{xy+y+z}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1f51faf-c287-515f-9df7-2b6d99509772" :answer/id #uuid "f1f51faf-c287-515f-9df7-2b6d99509772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-967a8907-2231-5e99-a474-67c6bc6bd9f3" :answer/id #uuid "967a8907-2231-5e99-a474-67c6bc6bd9f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy+y+z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5b43aea-006a-5e99-8f2a-f05fd804cb3c" :answer/id #uuid "c5b43aea-006a-5e99-8f2a-f05fd804cb3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b743d271-bfde-5d80-a2b4-b7cbdaa6f1f5" :question/id #uuid "b743d271-bfde-5d80-a2b4-b7cbdaa6f1f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161206" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{(2z)^{2}+(y)^{2}+{(3{x}^{2})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨y,\\,-z,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2z,\\,y,\\,3{x}^{2}⟩}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+3{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+yz}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{3{x}^{3}+yz}{\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eea0f599-a5c9-5a71-bef4-9b89d6f5c991"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eea0f599-a5c9-5a71-bef4-9b89d6f5c991" :answer-field/id #uuid "eea0f599-a5c9-5a71-bef4-9b89d6f5c991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25f30ee3-665e-5e1b-aa22-c26247f952a9" "answer-7bde0ba6-3997-5ac1-85d6-17921e2bfa0e" "answer-07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" "answer-7e3835b0-1d08-5073-b2ca-5face96ff967" "answer-8af4099a-a790-588b-a510-ac4dbee0f11c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25f30ee3-665e-5e1b-aa22-c26247f952a9" :answer/id #uuid "25f30ee3-665e-5e1b-aa22-c26247f952a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-yz" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bde0ba6-3997-5ac1-85d6-17921e2bfa0e" :answer/id #uuid "7bde0ba6-3997-5ac1-85d6-17921e2bfa0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2yz+xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" :answer/id #uuid "07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+yz" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e3835b0-1d08-5073-b2ca-5face96ff967" :answer/id #uuid "7e3835b0-1d08-5073-b2ca-5face96ff967" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+yz-xz" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8af4099a-a790-588b-a510-ac4dbee0f11c" :answer/id #uuid "8af4099a-a790-588b-a510-ac4dbee0f11c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}-yz" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167128 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163846 :answer-field/correct 17592186163848 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4c6d18b-d618-53cd-9fce-a599b57a0c5a" :question/id #uuid "e4c6d18b-d618-53cd-9fce-a599b57a0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161186" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,y-x,\\,x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x+y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2593ab3d-fc5b-5105-99bf-0103c2b5420d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2593ab3d-fc5b-5105-99bf-0103c2b5420d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2593ab3d-fc5b-5105-99bf-0103c2b5420d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-958fb760-98a9-540e-9910-e0ef71c1de34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82ef2bb9-743f-5b02-a915-dfb52e0d086a" "answer-958fb760-98a9-540e-9910-e0ef71c1de34" "answer-9c9e92e2-eb3e-592a-a5c1-dd41305c03d7" "answer-4e65dddf-58c2-5f17-8235-bfb8e2243bfb" "answer-5df024a4-000b-5ecc-b480-68e2ad920b0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82ef2bb9-743f-5b02-a915-dfb52e0d086a" :answer/id #uuid "82ef2bb9-743f-5b02-a915-dfb52e0d086a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-958fb760-98a9-540e-9910-e0ef71c1de34" :answer/id #uuid "958fb760-98a9-540e-9910-e0ef71c1de34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c9e92e2-eb3e-592a-a5c1-dd41305c03d7" :answer/id #uuid "9c9e92e2-eb3e-592a-a5c1-dd41305c03d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e65dddf-58c2-5f17-8235-bfb8e2243bfb" :answer/id #uuid "4e65dddf-58c2-5f17-8235-bfb8e2243bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5df024a4-000b-5ecc-b480-68e2ad920b0f" :answer/id #uuid "5df024a4-000b-5ecc-b480-68e2ad920b0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6480541e-888b-58cb-a4eb-1a531ae87114" :question/id #uuid "6480541e-888b-58cb-a4eb-1a531ae87114" :question/math-academy-id "q-161197" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a  continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4},$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2y,\\,2x,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [y]_{0}^{1/4}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bf0284d-4596-58d3-b54a-194c38549624"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bf0284d-4596-58d3-b54a-194c38549624" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf0284d-4596-58d3-b54a-194c38549624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d77c0b45-5ae9-59ba-8063-f366351598fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31bdabd7-159e-575d-bb25-4c387ed44025" "answer-9b7efde4-f50f-5cc1-8aa7-c78fcd23996d" "answer-d77c0b45-5ae9-59ba-8063-f366351598fc" "answer-c52866a7-6394-5695-8e68-74afc198f090" "answer-118324c2-6877-51fd-9281-af0fdd0a2878"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31bdabd7-159e-575d-bb25-4c387ed44025" :answer/id #uuid "31bdabd7-159e-575d-bb25-4c387ed44025" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b7efde4-f50f-5cc1-8aa7-c78fcd23996d" :answer/id #uuid "9b7efde4-f50f-5cc1-8aa7-c78fcd23996d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d77c0b45-5ae9-59ba-8063-f366351598fc" :answer/id #uuid "d77c0b45-5ae9-59ba-8063-f366351598fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c52866a7-6394-5695-8e68-74afc198f090" :answer/id #uuid "c52866a7-6394-5695-8e68-74afc198f090" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-118324c2-6877-51fd-9281-af0fdd0a2878" :answer/id #uuid "118324c2-6877-51fd-9281-af0fdd0a2878" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-49459c91-bfac-5582-aa3d-bc1bfea60a7b" :question/id #uuid "49459c91-bfac-5582-aa3d-bc1bfea60a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162157" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+x,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=-x+2y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=2\\sqrt{3}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=-x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=-x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+x,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2y,\\,x+y,\\,2x+6⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2y-2(x+y)+2x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27f85e1d-9f83-541a-b342-23041c17ed8f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27f85e1d-9f83-541a-b342-23041c17ed8f" :answer-field/id #uuid "27f85e1d-9f83-541a-b342-23041c17ed8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-351f00b6-bf80-5f84-ad64-40be3bbe2fd6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33f99146-c5ea-58b5-9bc1-58895cc4fc3f" "answer-7cb7492c-b0fb-5b7c-a8dc-51bc27779fe3" "answer-74c21c89-a987-5d09-9152-140d29002bac" "answer-351f00b6-bf80-5f84-ad64-40be3bbe2fd6" "answer-5e434de3-deea-53ed-838e-cf948a5ae65a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33f99146-c5ea-58b5-9bc1-58895cc4fc3f" :answer/id #uuid "33f99146-c5ea-58b5-9bc1-58895cc4fc3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cb7492c-b0fb-5b7c-a8dc-51bc27779fe3" :answer/id #uuid "7cb7492c-b0fb-5b7c-a8dc-51bc27779fe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74c21c89-a987-5d09-9152-140d29002bac" :answer/id #uuid "74c21c89-a987-5d09-9152-140d29002bac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-351f00b6-bf80-5f84-ad64-40be3bbe2fd6" :answer/id #uuid "351f00b6-bf80-5f84-ad64-40be3bbe2fd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e434de3-deea-53ed-838e-cf948a5ae65a" :answer/id #uuid "5e434de3-deea-53ed-838e-cf948a5ae65a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa928e5a-70af-5b00-a980-606761f10bda" :question/id #uuid "aa928e5a-70af-5b00-a980-606761f10bda" :question/math-academy-id "q-161243" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=3,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨1,\\,-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-\\frac{y}{2}+3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2y,\\,2z-x-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,(2x-y+6)-x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,2y,\\,x-2y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x-2y-2(x-2y+6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{3}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{3}}(2y-12)\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-6y]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be4704df-0892-5011-9881-9914abd2c673"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be4704df-0892-5011-9881-9914abd2c673" :answer-field/id #uuid "be4704df-0892-5011-9881-9914abd2c673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bdd1bd4e-9eca-533a-bc90-7301652e24d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dcd79be2-e35a-5ca5-821c-28d7b26f68a1" "answer-55f614dd-3b95-57de-8bba-fbdf8531494d" "answer-bdd1bd4e-9eca-533a-bc90-7301652e24d6" "answer-8a6976ed-177a-5352-84ae-a730d2e02dc6" "answer-6ee0e41b-d6ea-5017-8c8a-6f46a0e3dbdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcd79be2-e35a-5ca5-821c-28d7b26f68a1" :answer/id #uuid "dcd79be2-e35a-5ca5-821c-28d7b26f68a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f614dd-3b95-57de-8bba-fbdf8531494d" :answer/id #uuid "55f614dd-3b95-57de-8bba-fbdf8531494d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdd1bd4e-9eca-533a-bc90-7301652e24d6" :answer/id #uuid "bdd1bd4e-9eca-533a-bc90-7301652e24d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a6976ed-177a-5352-84ae-a730d2e02dc6" :answer/id #uuid "8a6976ed-177a-5352-84ae-a730d2e02dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ee0e41b-d6ea-5017-8c8a-6f46a0e3dbdb" :answer/id #uuid "6ee0e41b-d6ea-5017-8c8a-6f46a0e3dbdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167131 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z+3⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x+y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{3}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+y+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-x-y+(x+y+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163864 :answer-field/correct 17592186163869 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f0f5aff-a329-55d0-a382-ebd7a7992774" :question/id #uuid "2f0f5aff-a329-55d0-a382-ebd7a7992774" :question/math-academy-id "q-162548" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x-4y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 4.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-4y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,4,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-4y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨x-4y+1,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(x-4y+1-4(x+1)+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(-3x-3y-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy+x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2y+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3[{y}^{2}+4y]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =-96\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f22f763-7742-563a-8020-3b6c5519f8c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f22f763-7742-563a-8020-3b6c5519f8c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f22f763-7742-563a-8020-3b6c5519f8c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-974560cd-4e8d-59bd-8077-c163da225937" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cae5a31-54ec-52d7-9829-e6f79d0db7b0" "answer-228c1e6f-6068-550e-b050-12aa6caa4351" "answer-974560cd-4e8d-59bd-8077-c163da225937" "answer-52b6e1b8-f74d-549f-80b2-b5dec0190106" "answer-03158da1-b1d2-5e08-9fb3-bcf52d0b7ade"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cae5a31-54ec-52d7-9829-e6f79d0db7b0" :answer/id #uuid "3cae5a31-54ec-52d7-9829-e6f79d0db7b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-228c1e6f-6068-550e-b050-12aa6caa4351" :answer/id #uuid "228c1e6f-6068-550e-b050-12aa6caa4351" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-974560cd-4e8d-59bd-8077-c163da225937" :answer/id #uuid "974560cd-4e8d-59bd-8077-c163da225937" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52b6e1b8-f74d-549f-80b2-b5dec0190106" :answer/id #uuid "52b6e1b8-f74d-549f-80b2-b5dec0190106" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03158da1-b1d2-5e08-9fb3-bcf52d0b7ade" :answer/id #uuid "03158da1-b1d2-5e08-9fb3-bcf52d0b7ade" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167130 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y-2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+2y-2$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-2,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=2.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y-2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,y,\\,2x+2y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2y-(2x+2y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163858 :answer-field/correct 17592186163863 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168147 :tutorial/content "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\n \n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S,$ we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S.$\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\n \n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation. \n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\n \n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together. \n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side. \n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168146 :tutorial/content "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\n \n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n,$ which we write as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\n is given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S.\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields: \n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P.$\n\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168148 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166981 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
